<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Bus Arbitration</title>
    <style>
        * {
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        body {
            margin: 0;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            color: #172033;
            background: #f3f6fb;
        }

        header {
            padding: 48px 20px;
            text-align: center;
            color: white;
            background: #173b70;
        }

        header h1 {
            margin: 0 0 10px;
            font-size: 2.5rem;
        }

        header p {
            margin: 0;
            font-size: 1.1rem;
        }

        main {
            width: min(1000px, 92%);
            margin: 30px auto;
        }

        section {
            margin-bottom: 24px;
            padding: 24px;
            border-radius: 12px;
            background: white;
            box-shadow: 0 3px 12px #17203312;
        }

        h2 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: #173b70;
        }

        .methods {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
            gap: 16px;
        }

        .method {
            padding: 18px;
            border: 1px solid #dce4f0;
            border-radius: 10px;
        }

        .method h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: #245da0;
        }

        .bus-diagram {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            gap: 12px;
            margin: 20px 0 8px;
        }

        .device, .bus {
            padding: 12px 16px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            text-align: center;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .device {
            background: #e5efff;
        }

        .bus {
            color: white;
            background: #245da0;
        }

        .arrow {
            color: #245da0;
            font-size: 1.4rem;
        }

        footer {
            padding: 20px;
            text-align: center;
            color: #526078;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <h1>Bus Arbitration</h1>
        <p>How computer components take turns using a shared bus</p>
    </header>

    <main>
        <section>
            <h2>What is bus arbitration?</h2>
            <p>
                A computer bus is a shared communication path that connects components
                such as the processor, memory, and input/output devices. If more than
                one component requests the bus at the same time, bus arbitration
                decides which one may use it first.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>How it works</h2>
            <p>
                Devices send requests to an arbiter. The arbiter selects one requester
                and grants it control of the bus. After the transfer finishes, another
                device can be selected.
            </p>

            <div class="bus-diagram" aria-label="Devices request access through an arbiter">
                <div class="device">CPU</div>
                <div class="device">Device 1</div>
                <div class="device">Device 2</div>
                <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
                <div class="bus">Arbiter → Shared Bus</div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Common arbitration methods</h2>
            <div class="methods">
                <article class="method">
                    <h3>Daisy-chain</h3>
                    <p>
                        A grant signal passes from one device to the next. The first
                        requesting device in the chain gets the bus. It is simple and
                        inexpensive, but devices later in the chain may wait longer.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="method">
                    <h3>Centralized parallel</h3>
                    <p>
                        Devices send request signals to a central arbiter, which chooses
                        a requester and sends back a grant. It can be fast, but needs
                        more hardware and a central control point.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article class="method">
                    <h3>Distributed</h3>
                    <p>
                        Devices cooperate to decide who gets the bus instead of relying
                        on one central arbiter. It can avoid a single control point,
                        but is more complex to design.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Why is arbitration important?</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Prevents two devices from controlling the bus at once.</li>
                <li>Organizes requests from multiple components.</li>
                <li>Helps computer systems transfer data reliably.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
        Student project: Computer Organization and Architecture
    </footer>
</body>
</html>